Fix a use-after-free when an ACK arrives as its retransmit timer fires.

IHS_RetransmissionCancel took the pending packet out of the queue, then read it and stopped
its timer task. If the timer fired in between, its end callback freed the packet, and Cancel
then wrote into freed memory and stopped a task the timer thread had already freed (that memory
was usually reused at once by the next retransmission). On Wi-Fi the round trip is about the 10
ms retransmit interval, and a controller sends 250 reliable reports a second, so during play it
can happen many times a minute. A stress test under ASan hits it in every run.

A pending packet now belongs to its timer task: the end callback takes it out of the queue and
frees it, on the timer thread only. An ACK only marks it under the lock (found with the new
IHS_QueueFindBy, which leaves the item in place), and the task skips the resend. The item is
in the queue before its task starts.

